{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>Separate yourselves from the midst, etc.</b> It is not about Moses and Aaron that He speaks, for upon them the attribute of judgment would not strike even though they might be within the congregation that is being destroyed, Heaven forbid. And this is your sign — Aaron, who was in the midst of Korah's congregation, and the earth split beneath them and he was not struck. Rather, the intent of HaShem's statement concerns the righteous who were within the congregation — Joshua and Caleb and the rest of the righteous, and the members of the households of Moses and Aaron and of the rest of the righteous. Therefore, before the statement <b>Separate yourselves</b>, He said <b>and HaShem spoke to Moses</b>, etc., <b>saying</b>. And if He were speaking about Moses and Aaron, He would not have needed the word <b>saying</b>; rather, it is to say to the congregation of the righteous who were tested alongside them, and to their children and the members of their households who were within the congregation." "<b>And I will consume them in a moment.</b> One must know why He said <b>in a moment (כְּרָגַע)</b>. And if it is to inform that His power is great enough to kill six hundred thousand in an instant, He had already informed this previously, as it is written (Exodus 33:5): ״רֶגַע אֶחָד אֶעֱלֶה בְקִרְבְּךָ וַכִלִּיתִיךָ״." "And it appears according to the words of the Midrash (Numbers Rabbah) that Rashi brought on the verse ״וַיִּפֹּל עַל פָּנָיו״ (Numbers 22:4): this is the fourth [offense] by their hand: the calf, the complainers, the spies, etc. — end quote. If so, the sin of the spies preceded the dispute of Korah, and there I explained that the decree of destruction that HaShem decreed upon that generation was fulfilled, except that Moses' prayer availed that the people would not die all at once but over a long period of time. If so, the congregation was already liable to destruction. Therefore HaShem's statement comes here as a renewal of the decree, that it be <b>כְּרָגַע</b> — meaning, that He would not prolong their time as He had previously consented to do. And by this approach we find satisfaction for the difficulty in the matter: why would HaShem decree death upon the congregation of Israel when they had no hand in the transgression, as is revealed from Moses' statement nearby. And according to our words, since Israel were already liable to death from the sin of the spies, but HaShem had prolonged their time — and they said, their memory for blessing (Shabbat 32a), that the Satan prosecutes at a time of danger, and they instructed that whoever has the ability to converse between Yishtabach and Yotzer should withdraw from the ranks of the warring host (Jerusalem Talmud Berakhot 5:1) — even though the sin is not sufficient to warrant death. And likewise in what is before us, since they were liable to death, they would not escape when the destroyer goes forth, and they would perish without hope." "<b>Again</b> I looked and saw that HaShem's words here are a remedy and healing for the congregation of Israel. For had HaShem not said <b>Separate yourselves from the midst of the congregation</b>, and the destroyer had gone forth, the righteous who were not included in the decree of the spies would have escaped, and all who were included in the decree of the spies — the destroyer would have consumed them. And for HaShem to tell Israel to separate themselves so that no evil befall them — they did not have merit for this, since after all there was in their hand some small measure of sin, in that they did not protest against Korah, and on the contrary they came to his liking. Therefore HaShem was wise and commanded Moses and Aaron to separate the righteous and to arouse them to seek mercy upon them; and through the merit of Moses, HaShem said to him <b>Speak to the congregation</b>, etc., <b>Get you up from about</b>, etc. (Numbers 16:24). And by this approach we can explain that HaShem's statement to Moses and Aaron <b>Separate yourselves</b> — the divine speech came only to arouse them to pray, not that Moses needed to separate himself lest he be added to the sin of Korah. And perhaps this is what is alluded to in the word <b>לֵאמֹר</b> — meaning, that the purpose of the statement spoken in this matter is לֵאמֹר, to say, the statement that comes after it, which is the matter of separating Israel from Korah's congregation, for as regards Moses and Aaron there is no need to separate. And accordingly we do not need what we explained, that the divine speech concerned the members of their households and the righteous like them."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:10:29.153296543Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}